Q: Why class method argument can be narrower than that of interface A: <p>This is deliberate design and intended behavior due to <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/type-compatibility.html#function-parameter-bivariance" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Function Parameter Bivariance</a>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When comparing the types of function parameters, assignment succeeds if either the source parameter is assignable to the target parameter, or vice versa.  [...]</p>
</blockquote>
<p><sub><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/type-compatibility.html#function-parameter-bivariance" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/type-compatibility.html#function-parameter-bivariance</a></sub></p>
<p>Essentially, this means that function types are checked in both ways. The class implementation of <code>foo</code> is not only assignable to the declaration in <code>IFoo</code> if its type is more <em>narrow</em> but also if it's <em>wider</em>. A type error only occurs if  neither the interface declaration nor the class type implementation are assignable to one another.</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>interface IFoo {
  foo(a: string | number): void;
}

class Bar implements IFoo {
  foo(a: string) {}
}

class Baz implements IFoo {
  foo(a: string | number | boolean) {}
}

class Invalid implements IFoo {
  foo(a: string | boolean) {}
//~~~ Property 'foo' in type 'Invalid' is not assignable...
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can prevent this by enabling <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#strictFunctionTypes" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>--strictFunctionTypes</code></a>.
However, there is another important thing to notice. The strict parameter type checking only works when declaring <code>foo</code> as an <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/Arrow_functions" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><em>arrow function</em></a> (<code>foo: (a: string | number) =&gt; void;</code>).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>During development of this feature, we discovered a large number of inherently unsafe class hierarchies, including some in the DOM. Because of this, the setting only applies to functions written in function syntax, not to those in method syntax.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><sub><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#strictFunctionTypes" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#strictFunctionTypes</a></sub></p>
<p>Now you get your expected parameter type checking:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>interface IFoo {
  foo: (a: string | number) =&gt; void; // &lt;- !! function syntax
}

class Bar implements IFoo {
  foo(a: string) { }
//~~~ Property 'foo' in type 'Bar' is not assignable...
}
</code></pre>

Q: Why does TypeScript allow implementation of method expecting number or string to only accept number? A: <p>Unfortunately this is one of the loopholes in type safety caused by the fact that method parameters are checked bi-variantly. This means that as long as there is a relationship between the  function parameters it doesn't matter  in which direction that relationship is.</p>
<p>Here's a link to the related TypeScript handbook section: <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/type-compatibility.html#function-parameter-bivariance" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Function Parameter Bivariance</a></p>
<p>The reasoning for this is explained in the <a href="https://github.com/Microsoft/TypeScript/pull/18654" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PR</a> that introduces strict function types (i.e. contravariant parameter types for function signatures) and is basically that if methods were checked contravariantly it would result in most generic types being invariant (so you couldn't assign <code>Array&lt;Cat&gt;</code> to <code>Array&lt;Animal&gt;</code>).</p>
<p>One solution is to avoid method signatures and use function signatures wherever possible:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>interface Character {
    applyDamage: (value : number) =&gt; number;
}
interface Humanoid extends Character {
    hp:number;
    applyDamage: (value: number|string) =&gt; number
}
class Monster implements Humanoid {
    hp:number = 10;
    applyDamage(v: number) { // error
        this.hp -= v;
        return v;
    }
}
const monster:Humanoid = new Monster(); // error
monster.applyDamage(&quot;hello&quot;);
console.log(monster.hp); // &lt;-- NaN
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Playground Link</a></p>
<p>If you want to learn more about variance, you can watch my presentation on it <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EInunOVRsUU" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>

Q: Typescript - Why implementation of the interface is not forced accordingly A: <p><strong>TLDR:</strong></p>

<p>To make this work, change <strong>method</strong> notation  </p>

<pre><code>interface IHandle&lt;T&gt; {
    handle(event: T): void;
}
</code></pre>

<p>to <strong>property with a function type</strong>  </p>

<pre><code>interface IHandle&lt;T&gt; {
    handle: (event: T) =&gt; void;
}
</code></pre>

<p><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/index.html#" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Playground</a></p>

<p>** In this specific case implementation can still use method syntax</p>

<hr>

<p>Turns out this works as intended:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p><code>--strictFunctionTypes</code> mode in which function type parameter positions are checked contravariantly instead of bivariantly. The stricter checking applies to all function types, <strong>except those originating in method or construcor declarations</strong>. Methods are excluded specifically to ensure generic classes and interfaces (such as Array) continue to mostly relate covariantly. The impact of strictly checking methods would be a much bigger breaking change as a large number of generic types would become invariant (even so, we may continue to explore this stricter mode)</p>
</blockquote>

<p><a href="https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/pull/18654" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Source</a>  </p>

<p>Also in <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-2-6.html#strict-function-types" rel="nofollow noreferrer">handbook</a></p>

Q: Is there a way to analyze a PostgreSQL query to make sure that the result only contains columns from a single table? A: <p>For an inline-editable grid you need exactly this: every result column mapped unambiguously to the physical table column an edit would write back to. That is a column-lineage question, and <a href="https://github.com/NiclasOlofsson/sqllens" rel="nofollow noreferrer">sqllens</a> (TypeScript/Node) computes the mapping statically. The same origins that gate the query also give you each cell's write-back target:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { analyze, Schema } from &quot;sqllens&quot;;

const schema = new Schema({
  users: { id: &quot;int&quot;, name: &quot;string&quot; },
  orders: { id: &quot;int&quot;, total: &quot;decimal&quot; },
});

function editableResult(sql) {
  const q = analyze(sql, &quot;postgres&quot;, { schema });
  if (q.errors &gt; 0) return null;
  const tables = new Set();
  const mapping = [];
  for (const col of q.lineage.all) {
    const proj = col.projection;
    // Star-expanded outputs carry no projection node; they are real columns by
    // construction. An explicit projection must be a bare column reference
    // (no aggregate or expression wrapping).
    if (proj &amp;&amp; !proj.isStar &amp;&amp; proj.expr.kind !== &quot;column&quot;) return null;
    if (col.origins.length !== 1) return null;
    const o = col.origins[0];
    tables.add(o.table.join(&quot;.&quot;));
    mapping.push({ resultColumn: col.output, table: o.table.join(&quot;.&quot;), column: o.column });
  }
  return tables.size === 1 ? mapping : null;
}

editableResult(&quot;SELECT * FROM users&quot;);
// [{ resultColumn: &quot;id&quot;, table: &quot;users&quot;, column: &quot;id&quot; },
//  { resultColumn: &quot;name&quot;, table: &quot;users&quot;, column: &quot;name&quot; }]
editableResult(&quot;SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users&quot;);    // null (aggregate, not a real column)
editableResult(&quot;SELECT id + 1 AS x FROM users&quot;); // null (expression)
editableResult(&quot;SELECT u.id, o.total FROM users u JOIN orders o ON o.id = u.id&quot;); // null (two tables)
</code></pre>
<p><code>SELECT *</code> works because the analysis expands it against the schema into the real column list first. I am the author of the library.</p>

Q: How to parse standard SQL (BigQuery) to get input columns and output columns A: <p>If a Node/TypeScript step is an option: <a href="https://github.com/NiclasOlofsson/sqllens" rel="nofollow noreferrer">sqllens</a> produces exactly this mapping, rather than leaving you to derive it from a raw parse tree. Using your example:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>import { analyze, Schema } from &quot;sqllens&quot;;

const schema = new Schema({
  table1: { a: &quot;int&quot; },
  table2: { a: &quot;int&quot;, b: &quot;int&quot; },
});

const q = analyze(
  `SELECT t1.a, t2.b, t1.a + t2.b AS c
   FROM table1 t1 JOIN table2 t2 ON t1.a = t2.a`,
  &quot;bigquery&quot;,
  { schema },
);

q.lineage.originsOf(&quot;a&quot;); // table1.a
q.lineage.originsOf(&quot;b&quot;); // table2.b
q.lineage.originsOf(&quot;c&quot;); // table1.a, table2.b
</code></pre>
<p>(For <code>c</code> that's <code>table2.b</code> rather than the <code>table1.b</code> in your expected output — the expression is <code>t1.a + t2.b</code>.)</p>
<p>The schema mapping plays the role ZetaSQL's catalog does; the input tables come from the same analysis (<code>q.scopes</code>), and <code>SELECT *</code> expands via <code>q.qualification</code>. I'm the author.</p>
